C A DOUBLE-precision decimal literal assigned to a SCALAR DOUBLE
C destination must preserve its full 56-bit hex-float fraction, not
C silently truncate to single precision. resolve_operand's own
C QUAL_LIT case defaults every literal to single precision (zeroing
C lsw) since the litfile's own type tag can't reliably distinguish
C single vs double (every literal is tagged LIT_FIXED regardless --
C confirmed via a real litfile dump) -- SASN's own widening step must
C re-derive the value from the literal table directly rather than
C merely flipping the double_precision flag on the already-truncated
C resolved scalar, or the low word is lost permanently. Expected
C value confirmed against both real gpc and yaGPC2.
 T:
 PROGRAM;
    DECLARE X DOUBLE;
    X = 1.4142135623730951;
    WRITE(6) X;
 CLOSE T;
